# Bank 0 (runs at 0x4000). Directives: L label, C line comment,
# B block header, D data range, F row width.
#
# It is the only FIXED bank in the cartridge: there is no register that changes
# it, so it is always at 0x4000-0x5FFF. That is why it holds everything that
# has to be available no matter what: the header, the boot, the interrupt hook,
# the dispatcher, the mapper routines and the game's state machine.
# ------------------------------------------------------------------ labels
# --------------------------------------------------------------------- notes
# ---------------------------------------------------------------- data
# ---------------------------------------------------- more named routines
# ------------------------------------------------- comments on the pieces
# THE INTERRUPT
# INIT
# THE DISPATCHER
# THE TWO ADDITIONS
# THE DECOMPRESSOR
# THE OTHER CARTRIDGE
# THE STATE MACHINE
# ------------------------------------------------------- the sprite engine
B 0x4000 CARTRIDGE HEADER
D 0x4000 0x4010 header  MSX cartridge header: "AB", INIT = 0x4071, and the other three entries (STATEMENT, DEVICE, TEXT) at zero, plus six reserved bytes.
F 0x4000 2,w1,w1,w1,w1,6
# --- The second header, the Game Master's. No instruction in the cartridge
# reads it: it is read by the OTHER cartridge, Konami's Game Master, which
# plugs into the slot next door and needs to know where this one keeps its
# things so it can tamper with them. "CD" format: mark, catalogue number, a
# flags byte and only the fields that byte announces. The 19 bytes the Game
# Master copies start at 0x4012, and the count closes exactly at 0x4025.
D 0x4010 0x4012 gm_mark  The format mark: "CD" in ASCII (43 44). It is what the Game Master looks for to know that this cartridge carries the long header; the ones that carry the short one have "AB".
F 0x4010 2
D 0x4012 0x4014 gm_catalogue  The catalogue number in BCD, high byte first: 07 42, that is RC-742, which is this cartridge's.
F 0x4012 2
D 0x4014 0x4015 gm_flags  0x80: says which fields follow. They are read from bit 0 to 7 and a CLEAR bit means "this field is in the stream". Here the first seven are present and the last one is missing, which is a callback through CALSLT.
F 0x4014 1
D 0x4015 0x4025 gm_fields  The seven fields the flags byte announces, in bit order: 0xE000 with the 0x04 that says when there is a live game (0x53B1 state_4 is where the stage starts); 0xE061 the stage with its modulo 8 (0x4129); 0xE060 the lives (0x53CC, in BCD); 0xE053 the high score; 0xE05B one player's score and 0xE057 the other's (0x54AA picks between the two by bit 7 of 0xE002 and clears four bytes); and 0xE002, the game flags. That 0xE053 is the high score is something the cartridge itself tells us: 0x5558 clears from 0xE057 up to 0xEFFF when a game starts and those four bytes are exactly the ones it leaves standing.
F 0x4015 3,3,w1
L 0x4025 jump_to_intro   `jp 0x5A54`. It is the only place that enters the intro.
B 0x4028 THE INTERRUPT
L 0x4028 interrupt   What INIT installs in the H.KEYI hook (0xFD9A). THE WHOLE GAME RUNS HERE.
C 0x4028 Reading the VDP status is what acknowledges the interrupt.
C 0x402C Bank 7 at 0x8000 and bank 8 at 0xA000: the sound player and its data.
C 0x402E NOTE: the RAM copy at 0xF0F2 is NOT touched here, and that is why it can be undone afterwards.
C 0x4035 The sound runs entirely inside the interrupt.
C 0x4039 Whatever bank layout was there before is restored, reading it from the RAM copy.
C 0x4045 Lock: if the game loop is already inside, this interrupt does not enter again.
C 0x404D The `ei` goes BEFORE the long work: the next interrupt can catch it halfway.
C 0x4051 And here the real game starts.
C 0x4055 The lock is released.
L 0x405D add_a_to_hl   HL += A, with the carry done right. Called from 48 places.
C 0x405D HL = HL + A, fixing H if there is a carry.
L 0x4062 add_a_to_de   DE += A. Called from 26 places.
C 0x4062 DE = DE + A, the same.
B 0x4067 THE KONAMI DISPATCHER
L 0x4067 dispatcher   Konami's dispatcher: the destination table goes RIGHT BEHIND the `call`.
C 0x4067 `pop hl` takes the RETURN address, which is where the table starts.
C 0x4068 Each entry is two bytes, so the index is doubled.
C 0x406C The word in the table is the destination...
C 0x4070 ...and here is the jump. The caller does NOT return: the table eats the return address.
B 0x4071 INIT: THE BOOT
L 0x4071 INIT   What the BIOS calls at boot (the AB header says so at 0x4002).
C 0x4072 Interrupt mode 1: the BIOS hook at 0x0038 ends up leading to 0xFD9A.
C 0x4076 0xF0F1, 0xF0F2 and 0xF0F3 are the RAM copy of the three mapper registers.
C 0x407B Bank 1 at 0x6000...
C 0x4080 ...bank 2 at 0x8000...
C 0x4086 ...and bank 3 at 0xA000. It is the default layout for the whole game.
C 0x4090 H = 0x80: this cartridge is enabled in page 2, which is where 0x8000 and 0xA000 fall.
C 0x4095 `jp interrupt` is installed in the H.KEYI hook.
C 0x40A0 The stack, right below the mapper copy.
C 0x40A3 The 4 KB from 0xE000 to 0xEFFF are cleared, which is all of the game's memory.
C 0x40B2 0xE005 and 0xE006 to 1: the lock is set while booting.
C 0x40C5 And once again the usual layout, now with the RAM clean
C 0x40DE INIT ends here, and stays here: the `jr $` next to it never exits.
C 0x40DF And this is the end of INIT. There is no way out of here: the rest happens in the interrupt.
L 0x40E1 find_my_slot   Builds for ENASLT the slot number of this cartridge, reading RSLREG and SLTTBL.
C 0x40E1 RSLREG: the primary slot register
C 0x40E4 Two rotations: the slot of page 1, which is where this cartridge sits
C 0x40EB 0xFCC1: the BIOS table that says whether that slot has subslots
C 0x40F0 Bit 7 marks that it has them
C 0x40F4 Four more: the subslot of page 1
L 0x40FD jump_to_request_sound   `jp 0x49E9`. Nobody jumps here: it is dead code.
D 0x40FD 0x4100 dead_jump  Three bytes that are `jp 0x49E9`, that is, a shortcut to request a sound. No instruction or table in the cartridge points to 0x40FD, and the one before ends in `ret`: it is dead code, and that is why it is listed as bytes.
F 0x40FD 3
B 0x4100 STARTING A GAME AND STARTING A STAGE
L 0x4100 start_game   Sets the score to zero, clears the 0x900 bytes of objects at 0xE300 and loads the stage data
C 0x4100 The score labels
C 0x4107 0xE071 to zero: the power-up count
C 0x410A 0xE108, 0xE063 and 0xE127 to zero, two bytes at a time
C 0x4116 The 0x900 bytes of the object table, to zero
C 0x4123 And the data of whichever stage is due
L 0x4129 set_up_due_stage   Does NOT advance the stage: with 0xE061 at zero it sets it to one, and from the ninth onwards it replaces it with the one the table at 0x418F says; then it sets it up
C 0x412C With the stage at zero, start with the first one
C 0x4133 Below nine the stage is set up as it is
C 0x4137 The table at 0x418F, indexed by the stage minus nine: it only has four entries
C 0x4141 And the power-up count and the stage counter, to zero
L 0x414B start_stage   Clears the 0xE00 bytes at 0xE100, turns off the 128 sprites, loads the stage data and waits 0x3C frames
C 0x414E The 0xE00 bytes from 0xE100 onwards, to zero
C 0x415B 0xE0 in the 128 Ys of the sprite buffer: none is drawn
C 0x416D Sixteen bytes from 0x4193 to 0xE200
C 0x417B 0x3C frames of waiting before starting
L 0x4183 flag_if_two_players   0xE130 to one if 0xE06B is not zero
D 0x418F 0x41A3 stages_per_round  Only FOUR useful bytes, from 0x418F to 0x4192: 0x03, 0x04, 0x05 and 0x08. They are indexed by 0x4137 with `ld de,0x418F` + 0x4062 and the index is (0xE061 minus 9), so they say which stage comes after 9, 10, 11 and 12. There is NO fifth entry: the byte at 0x4193 is already the first of the SIXTEEN that 0x416D copies to 0xE200 (the record the ship starts with).
F 0x418F 4
L 0x41A3 stage_difficulty   0xE111 comes from the round times four plus the stage, capped at 0x0F; past the ninth, from 0xE066
C 0x41A6 From the ninth onwards, the difficulty is set by 0xE066
C 0x41AC The round times four
C 0x41B2 Capped at 0x0F
L 0x41C3 load_stage_data   Six bytes from the table at 0x4499 to 0xE101 (where the map script starts and ends), and from the one at 0x4212 the checkpoint the counter is compared with
C 0x41C6 Times six: six bytes per stage
C 0x41D0 To 0xE101
C 0x41DB The other table, the checkpoints one
C 0x41E4 DCOMPR: if you had already passed the checkpoint, it starts there; and if not, at 0x20
L 0x41EA set_stage_counters   Sets the stage's dozen counters to zero, 0xE062 to one and 0xE129 to 0x40
C 0x41EA The distance the stage starts with
C 0x420A 0xE062 to one
C 0x420E 0xE129 starts at 0x40
D 0x4212 0x422A checkpoints  ELEVEN words, from 0x4214 to 0x422A: the CHECKPOINT of each stage. It is read by 0x41DB with `ld hl,0x4212` and 0x47AE, which adds the stage TWICE, so stage 1 reads the one at 0x4214 and stage 11 the one at 0x4228. When setting up the stage, 0x41E1 compares the distance already covered with this number; if you had passed it, the stage restarts right there, and if not, at 0x20. They are 0xF8, 0x100, 0xF2, 0x100, 0x100, 0x100, 0xF8, 0x100, 0x100, 0x100 and 0x100. NOTE: STAGE 12 RUNS OFF THE TABLE. Its read lands at 0x422A, which are the first two bytes of the `call 0x58BA` at 0x422A, and gives it 0xBACD = 47821; since the distance never gets anywhere near that, stage 12 always starts at 0x20.
F 0x4212 w2
B 0x422A THE GRAPHICS OF EACH STAGE
B 0x422A The cartridge does not store the screens already drawn: it stores
B 0x422A compressed blocks in banks 4, 5 and 6, and when a stage starts it
B 0x422A decompresses them into VRAM. With the graphics in place, 0x4348 ALSO
B 0x422A makes the flipped characters: 0x43F2 reverses the bits of each byte
B 0x422A (horizontal mirror) and 0x43C6 reverses the order of the eight bytes
B 0x422A (vertical mirror). That way a drawing and its reflection take up a
B 0x422A single block in the ROM.
L 0x422A load_stage_graphics   Maps banks 4, 5 and 6, decompresses what is due for the stage and restores the usual layout
C 0x422D Banks 4, 5 and 6, which are the graphics ones
C 0x422E Banks 4, 5 and 6, which are the graphics ones
C 0x4249 The block at 0x86BB to VRAM 0x1800: the sprite patterns
C 0x4252 The stage times six: two groups of three bytes in the table at 0x42AD
C 0x425F Two blocks per stage
L 0x4261 decompress_both_blocks   For each group: the source in DE and, in C, the character where it starts; the VRAM address comes from C times eight plus 0x1800
C 0x4266 The third byte is the character where it starts
C 0x4269 Times eight: each character is eight bytes
C 0x426F And from VRAM 0x1800
C 0x427D Stage 5 also gets the block at 0x8FCB at VRAM 0x1D00
C 0x428A And with 0xF0F4 set, one more at 0x1800
L 0x4299 restore_usual_layout   Banks 1, 2 and 3 in their three slots, and the RAM copy up to date
C 0x4299 The usual layout is restored: banks 1, 2 and 3
C 0x42A0 Bank 1 at 0x6000...
C 0x42A5 ...bank 2 at 0x8000...
C 0x42AB ...and bank 3 at 0xA000
D 0x42B3 0x42FB graphics_per_stage  Six bytes per stage: two groups of (source of the compressed block, index of the character where it starts). The declared base is 0x42AD (0x4259), which falls INSIDE the code: stage 0 does not exist and the table really starts at stage 1. The sources point into bank 5.
F 0x42B3 6
D 0x42FB 0x42FC empty_block  A single 0x00. It is a compressed stream that ends at its first byte, and stages 9 to 12 use it as "there is no second block here".
F 0x42FB 1
L 0x42FC load_stage_characters   The entries at 0x932D and the ones due for the stage, plus the two batches of mirrors
C 0x42FC The entries at 0x932D: the ones every stage gets
C 0x4306 And the table at 0x92E3, indexed by the stage
C 0x4313 0xE100 to zero: the batch that is flipped by bits
C 0x431F And to one: the one that is flipped by bytes
C 0x4326 The 0xF0F4 part is only loaded if it is set
C 0x4335 From the ninth stage onwards, one more set
L 0x433F walk_entries   Takes from the table in HL the list due for the stage and walks it
L 0x4348 build_mirror   For each four-byte entry: unpacks it, flips it and uploads it to the thirds its bits say
C 0x4348 A zero first byte ends the list
C 0x434D The fourth byte is the character; times eight, the bytes it takes up
C 0x435B 0xE100 says which mirror it is
C 0x4360 At zero, the bit mirror: horizontal
C 0x4364 And at one, the byte mirror: vertical
C 0x436A Four bytes per entry
L 0x4371 load_entries   Walks six-byte entries and passes the patterns and colours of each one to 0x49B9.
C 0x4376 Bit 0: the third third, at VRAM 0x1000
C 0x437D Bit 1: the middle one, at 0x0800
C 0x4387 And bit 2: the first one, at 0x0000
C 0x4391 Six bytes per entry
L 0x4398 decompress_patterns_and_colours   The character times eight plus the third: the patterns go 0x2000 higher and the colours as they are
C 0x4398 The character times eight, plus the third that is due
C 0x43A2 The patterns live 0x2000 above the colours
C 0x43A6 The pointer to the patterns, in the entry
C 0x43B8 And the one to the colours, three bytes further on
L 0x43C6 flip_bytes   VERTICAL mirror: reverses the order of the character's eight bytes, in the two buffers at 0xE300 and 0xE700
C 0x43C6 The pattern buffer...
C 0x43CF ...and the colour one
C 0x43D6 As many characters as the entry says
C 0x43DA Four swaps: the eight bytes reversed
L 0x43DC swap_eight_bytes   The character's eight bytes, swapped in pairs working inwards: that is the vertical mirror
C 0x43DC One goes up and the other goes down
C 0x43E5 Four more: the next character
C 0x43E9 0x0C: the next character, counting backwards
L 0x43F2 flip_bits   HORIZONTAL mirror: reverses the eight bits of each byte with `rr (hl)` and `adc a,a`
C 0x43F9 Eight bits per byte
C 0x43FB Out at the bottom and in at the top: the byte reversed
C 0x4402 As many bytes as 0xE101 says
L 0x4408 upload_mirror   Bits 3, 4 and 5 of the second byte say which thirds the flipped character is uploaded to
C 0x4408 Bit 3: the third third
C 0x4412 Bit 4: the middle one
C 0x441C And bit 5: the first one
L 0x4424 upload_mirror_to_vram   The flipped character goes back to VRAM: the patterns from 0xE300 and the colours from 0xE700
C 0x4426 The second byte of the entry is the destination character
C 0x442B Times eight, plus the third that DE brings
C 0x4430 The patterns, 0x2000 higher
C 0x4437 And as many bytes as 0xE101 says
C 0x443F The colours go as they are, without the 0x2000
L 0x4450 download_character_to_ram   The character to be flipped is brought from VRAM to 0xE300 and 0xE700: to make the mirror you have to read what was already uploaded
C 0x4454 The first byte of the entry: the source character
C 0x4460 Bit 2: the first third, at 0x2000
C 0x4466 Bit 1: the middle one, at 0x2800
C 0x446E And if not, the third one, at 0x3000
C 0x4476 LDIRMV: from VRAM to RAM, the reverse of LDIRVM
C 0x447F The same three thirds for the colours: 0x0000, 0x0800 and 0x1000
D 0x449F 0x44E7 stage_positions  Rows of six bytes that 0x41CA copies to 0xE101 with the base 0x4499, again six bytes ahead of where the range starts.
F 0x449F 6
B 0x44E7 THE PAUSE
L 0x44E7 check_pause_key   With the game running, looks at bit 5 of keyboard row 6 and, when it is pressed, toggles between pause and play
C 0x44E7 Bit 6 of 0xE002: only with the game running
C 0x44EE And with 0xE1D0 at zero
C 0x44F6 SNSMAT of row 6; bit 5 is the GRAPH key
C 0x44F9 A cpl: on the keyboard a pressed key is a zero
C 0x44FC 0xE10C keeps the previous state, to catch the new press
C 0x4501 What has changed and is set: it has just gone down
C 0x4506 0xE10B counts the presses: even is play, odd is pause
C 0x4509 In play, 0xE047 to zero
C 0x450E And in pause, 0x0101
C 0x4518 Only with the game paused are the keyboard cheats read
C 0x451F 0xE200 negative: the pause is handled elsewhere
C 0x4526 Three routines from banks 2 and 3, with the game stopped
B 0x4532 ONE FRAME OF PLAY
B 0x4532 This is the game's shopping list: thirty-odd calls in a row, nearly all
B 0x4532 of them to banks 1, 2 and 3, and everything that happens in a frame is
B 0x4532 done in that order. There is no loop and no table: it is a strip of
B 0x4532 `call`s. Banks 11 and 12 (the pieces and the stage scripts) are slipped
B 0x4532 in the middle on purpose for a single call, and then 2 and 3 are
B 0x4532 restored.
L 0x4532 game_frame   The strip of calls that makes a whole frame: the scroll, the enemies, the shots, the collisions and the score
C 0x4532 0xE009 is copied to 0xE10D: the joystick, as it was left
C 0x453E With 0xE1D0 set there is an explosion in progress
C 0x4545 And with 0xE1D1 set, the frame is cut short here
C 0x454E 0xE112 to zero
C 0x4551 Banks 11 and 12: the pieces and the stage scripts
C 0x4557 The RAM copy of the mapper is updated at the same time
C 0x4565 And with them in place, the piece of screen that is due is built
C 0x4568 2 and 3 restored, which are the usual ones
C 0x45EB One frame in eight
C 0x45F2 ...it is time to refresh the score
L 0x45F5 start_scroll   Moves the distance counter back 0x20 and leaves the screen pointer at 0xED00, with 0x20 steps to take
C 0x45F6 0xEC04 to zero
C 0x45F9 0x20 less on the distance covered
C 0x4604 0xED00: where it starts reading the screen
C 0x460A Thirty-two steps
B 0x460C THE SCROLL
B 0x460C The stage map is not read from VRAM: it lives in RAM, at 0xED00, and it
B 0x460C is twenty-two rows of thirty-two cells. On each scroll step, 0x469D
B 0x460C SHIFTS IT ONE COLUMN TO THE LEFT with twenty-two `ldir`s of 0x1F bytes,
B 0x460C and 0x46AE puts the new column in on the right, which comes from the
B 0x460C stage script and from the 4x4-character pieces of bank 11.
L 0x460C take_scroll_steps   B scroll steps; on each one it maps banks 11 and 12 to read the script, and then four more routines
C 0x460E Banks 11 and 12: the pieces and the scripts
C 0x4621 With them in place the new column is read
C 0x4625 And 2 and 3 are restored
C 0x4644 0xEC00 moves forward one cell
C 0x464B 0xEC04, eight points per step
C 0x4652 And the distance covered, one unit
C 0x465C On the way out the last step is given back: the loop overshoots by one
L 0x4664 flag_not_due   0xE107 to one: no new column comes in on this step
L 0x466A scroll_map_one_column   If it is time to scroll, raises the distance and shifts the twenty-two rows at 0xED00 one cell to the left
C 0x4671 0xE105 is how far the stage's scroll goes
C 0x4678 DCOMPR: compares the distance with the limit
C 0x467B 0xE1C0 sets the speed: at one, standing still
C 0x4680 At two, it shifts on every step
C 0x4683 And otherwise, every other step, with the bit that rotates in 0xE062
C 0x468C The distance goes up
C 0x4690 0xE100 to one: there is a new column on this step
C 0x4695 From 0xED01 to 0xED00: the whole map, one cell to the left
C 0x469B Twenty-two rows
C 0x469E Thirty-one cells per row
C 0x46A4 And the leftover cell when jumping to the next row
C 0x46A8 0xEC00 points to the right-hand column, the one to be filled
L 0x46AE read_new_column   With the distance inside the range, takes from the stage script the piece that is due and leaves its address in 0xEC02
C 0x46AE The distance covered...
C 0x46B1 ...against the end of the range
C 0x46B9 And against where it starts
C 0x46C3 The script table at 0x97DE, indexed by the stage
C 0x46CE The distance divided by two: each piece is four cells wide
C 0x46D6 Times three and added: times six, which is what a row of the script takes up
C 0x46D9 0xEC02 keeps where the piece is
L 0x46E1 insert_new_column   Copies into the right-hand column of the map the four cells due from the piece, jumping from row to row
C 0x46E3 The script hands out piece numbers
C 0x46EE Times sixteen: each piece is four by four characters
C 0x46F2 Stages 5, 9, 10 and 12 use the other set of pieces, the one at 0x8FF0
C 0x470D The two low bits of the distance: which of the four columns of the piece is due
C 0x4717 0x20: from one map row to the one below
C 0x471F And four bytes: the same column, one row further down the piece
L 0x472A half_piece   The other two cells of the piece, when the column coming in is the second half
C 0x472A The third byte of the piece
C 0x472C Four bytes: the next row down in the piece
C 0x4733 And 0x20: the next row down in the map
B 0x4738 THE STARS COME FROM THE R REGISTER
B 0x4738 When the stage has no script to read, the column coming in is sky: a
B 0x4738 single character lit on the row the table at 0x478E says, and the rest
B 0x4738 at zero. Which of the two star drawings goes in is decided by `ld a,r`,
B 0x4738 the Z80's refresh register, which counts on its own with every
B 0x4738 instruction. It is the closest thing to chance in the cartridge, and it
B 0x4738 is only used so that the stars do not all twinkle the same.
L 0x4738 draw_star_column   Fills the incoming column with zeros, except for one cell: the star, on the row the table at 0x478E says
C 0x4738 The five low bits of the distance index the table
C 0x4743 C says which row the star falls on
C 0x4744 Twenty-two rows
C 0x4750 The Z80's R register: the only thing that stands in for chance here
C 0x4754 0xF6 or 0xF7: the two star drawings
L 0x475B blink_two_characters   Every two frames turns the patterns at 0x27B0 and 0x27B8 off or on, in the three thirds
C 0x475B Two bits of the frame counter: four steps
C 0x4760 The mask table at 0x4786
C 0x476A The character at VRAM 0x27B0...
C 0x4771 ...and the one at 0x27B8
C 0x4774 0xE062, the bit that rotates
C 0x477B The three thirds, 0x800 apart
D 0x4786 0x478E blink  Four words that 0x4760 indexes with (0xE003 AND 6).
F 0x4786 w2
D 0x478E 0x47AE star_row  Thirty-two bytes that 0x473D indexes with (0xE063 AND 0x1F): which of the twenty-two rows the star of the incoming column falls on. The values go from 1 to 0x14, so it always falls inside.
F 0x478E 32
L 0x47AE get_word   DE = the word at HL + 2*A. Called from 36 places.
C 0x47AE Times two: the table is made of words
L 0x47B7 run_sprites   Four passes: two routines from banks 1 and 3, the second group of objects and the sprite engine
L 0x47C3 upload_sprites   Sends the 128 bytes at 0xEC80 out of the VDP data port with `outi`: 32 sprites of four bytes, in one go.
C 0x47C8 `outi` with B=0x80: the 128 bytes of the attribute table without going through the BIOS.
B 0x47CE SPRITE PRIORITY, WHICH KEEPS ROTATING
B 0x47CE The MSX only draws four sprites per line, and the ones that drop out
B 0x47CE are always the last ones in the table. So that the one that disappears
B 0x47CE is not always the same, this routine does not upload the buffer in one
B 0x47CE go: it uploads it in THIRTY-TWO pieces of four bytes, starting each
B 0x47CE frame at a different place (0xE17F goes up by 0x1C and wraps at 0x7C)
B 0x47CE and stepping 0x0C at a time. That way each object lands in a different
B 0x47CE slot of the attribute table every frame, and the flicker is shared out
B 0x47CE among all of them.
L 0x47CE upload_sprites_rotating   Uploads the buffer to the attribute table starting each frame at a different place: that way the flicker is shared out
C 0x47CE The sprite attribute table, at VRAM 0x3B00
C 0x47D5 With 0xE200 at 0xFF it is uploaded as it is, without rotating
C 0x47DF 0x1C more each frame, wrapping at 0x7C
C 0x47E5 Thirty-two entries of four bytes
C 0x47EF The four bytes of an entry, through the data port
C 0x47F5 And on to the entry 0x0C further on, wrapping around
L 0x47FE upload_screen   The 704 bytes of the map at 0xED00 to the name table, with `outi` and without going through the BIOS
C 0x47FE The name table, at VRAM 0x3800
C 0x4808 B at zero: 256 bytes in one go
C 0x4814 And the last 192: twenty-two rows of thirty-two
L 0x481C build_second_group   The other ten objects, the ones at 0xE500, to the buffer at 0xECD8
C 0x481C 0xE500: the second group of objects
C 0x4823 Ten objects
B 0x4827 THE SPRITE ENGINE: FROM THE OBJECTS AT 0xE300 TO THE ATTRIBUTE TABLE
L 0x4827 build_sprites   With banks 4/5/6 in place, walks twelve objects at 0xE300 (32 bytes each) and builds their sprite attribute in 0xECA8.
C 0x482C Banks 4/5/6: the sprite engine needs to read the shapes in bank 5.
C 0x4840 0xE300 is the object table, and 0xECA8 the attribute buffer
L 0x4849 walk_objects   For each live slot, sets its attribute entry; and if it is hidden, takes it off the screen
C 0x484A With the first byte at zero, the slot is empty
C 0x4850 And 0x19 also counts as empty
C 0x4854 Byte 11 says whether the object is hidden
L 0x4860 next_object   Moves on to the next object and, once the twelve are done, restores the usual layout
C 0x4861 Thirty-two bytes per object
C 0x4868 The twelve
C 0x486A Banks 1, 2 and 3 again
C 0x486B The usual layout: banks 1, 2 and 3
L 0x4884 empty_slot   0xE0 in the Y: the VDP draws nothing below that height
C 0x4884 0xE0 in the Y and the other three fields untouched
L 0x488D set_attribute   Copies to the buffer the object's Y, X, pattern number and colour (IX+4, IX+6, IX+0C, IX+0D).
C 0x488D Byte 4 of the object is the Y...
C 0x4892 ...6 the X...
C 0x4897 ...12 the pattern number...
C 0x489C ...and 13 the colour
L 0x48A2 hide_sprite   Puts 0xE0 in the Y, which is the height at which the VDP does not draw it, and drops the object if it goes off the bottom or the right.
C 0x48A9 Below Y 0xB8 and X 0xF8 it carries on; otherwise, it is not drawn with characters
C 0x48B6 Bytes 30 and 31 say which screen cell it falls on
C 0x48BC Byte 11: if it is one it is four characters, and if not, two
L 0x48C3 two_by_two   Takes the object's four characters from the table at 0x91D1 and writes them to the name table in two rows, 0x1E apart.
C 0x48C3 The pattern number times four: four characters per drawing
C 0x48CF The top two...
C 0x48D3 ...and 0x1E further on, the bottom two, which is the next row
L 0x48E0 two_characters   Objects that are not four characters are painted with two in a row, and byte 11 says which one they start at
C 0x48E0 Byte 11 at two: characters 0xA1 and 0xA2
C 0x48E5 At three: 0xBF and 0xC0
C 0x48EA At four: 0x58 and 0x59
C 0x48EF And otherwise, 0x60 and 0x61
C 0x48F1 The second character goes right after the first
L 0x48F7 clear_rectangle   B cells wide by C high, to zero, clipping whatever goes off the right
C 0x48FD Zero: the empty cell
C 0x4903 0x20: the row below
L 0x490C copy_rectangle   The same but copying from DE: B wide by C high, skipping in the source whatever was clipped
C 0x4911 What was clipped, to skip it in the source too
L 0x4917 copy_row   B bytes from DE to HL: one row of the rectangle
C 0x4917 Byte by byte
C 0x491F 0x20: the map row below
L 0x492E clip_right   If the rectangle goes off the row, takes the leftover cells off B
C 0x4932 Which column it falls on
C 0x4935 And how far past thirty-two it goes
C 0x4938 B keeps what fits
L 0x493D clear_screen   Calls 0x55AA and fills the 768 bytes of the name table (VRAM 0x3800) with zeros using FILVRM.
C 0x4940 The 768 cells of the name table
L 0x494A set_vram_write   SETWRT, and the VDP data port is saved in C'.
C 0x494F 0x0007 holds this machine's VDP data port
L 0x4956 set_vram_read   SETRD, and the read port is saved in C'. Nobody calls it: it is the dead twin of 0x494A.
D 0x4956 0x4960 dead_vram_read  Ten bytes that are `call SETRD / exx / ld a,(0x0006) / ld c,a / exx / ret`: the twin of 0x494A for READING from VRAM. Nobody calls it.
F 0x4956 10
L 0x4960 dump_to_vram   LDIRVM with DE and HL swapped.
L 0x4964 dump_three_thirds   Three LDIRVMs in a row, adding 0x800 each time: the three thirds of the screen.
C 0x4965 Three thirds
C 0x4967 One third at a time
C 0x496D 0x800: the next third
L 0x4977 fill_three_thirds   Three FILVRMs, one per third.
C 0x4977 Three thirds
L 0x4988 decompress_three_thirds   0x49B9 three times, once per third.
C 0x4988 Three thirds
L 0x498A next_third   0x800 more: the third below
C 0x498A One third at a time
C 0x498F 0x800: the next third
L 0x4998 write_characters   Reads the VRAM address from the stream and writes characters with WRTVRM: 0xFE = another address follows, 0xFF = end. It is the simple, uncompressed sibling of 0x49B9.
C 0x4998 C to 0xFF: the `and c` below leaves the character as it is
C 0x499B The first two bytes are the VRAM cell
C 0x49A3 0xFF ends the message
C 0x49A5 And 0xFE continues it at another cell
C 0x49A8 This is where C decides between writing the character or a zero
L 0x49AF erase_characters   The same as 0x4998 but with C=0, so the `and c` leaves every character at zero: it is used to erase the text 0x4998 wrote.
L 0x49B3 decompress_with_destination   Enters the decompressor after first reading the VRAM address from the stream itself.
C 0x49B3 The first two bytes of the stream are the destination in VRAM
L 0x49B9 decompress   The graphics decompressor. See tools/rle.py.
C 0x49B9 HL brings the VRAM address and DE the compressed stream.
C 0x49BC Command byte.
C 0x49BE The 0x00 ends the block.
C 0x49C1 Bit 7 is split off, which is the one that says whether a literal or a repeat follows.
C 0x49C4 Bit 7 at zero: repeat the next byte.
C 0x49C7 The 0x80 command (bit 7 and nothing else) changes the VRAM address.
C 0x49CA Literal copy: B bytes as they are, sending them out of the VDP port.
C 0x49D4 Repeat: the same byte B times.
L 0x49DE check_if_sound   Checks bit 6 of 0xE002 before falling into the sound trigger.
C 0x49DE Bit 6 of 0xE002: with no game, nothing sounds
L 0x49E9 request_sound   Maps banks 7 and 8, writes sound A down in the queue, and restores the previous layout.
L 0x49EB build_sound_request   Maps banks 7 and 8, which are the player's, leaves the request in the queue and restores the previous layout
C 0x49EB Everything is saved: this is called from anywhere
C 0x49EF Bank 7 at 0x8000 and bank 8 at 0xA000
C 0x4A05 With them in place the request is queued
C 0x4A09 And 2 and 3 restored
L 0x4A22 queue_sound   Writes the request into the queue at 0xE012/0xE034 using the table at 0x8328 in bank 7.
C 0x4A23 The seven low bits are the sound number; bit 7 is separate
C 0x4A25 Two queue channels
C 0x4A2A Below 0x16, the sound goes to the 0xE034 queue
C 0x4A35 From 0x26 onwards, one more channel
C 0x4A3F What is already queued
C 0x4A46 If what is already playing weighs more, the request is thrown away
C 0x4A49 The table at 0x8328 in bank 7, indexed by two
L 0x4A51 write_request   Leaves in the channel's card the marker, the sound number and the two words that come out of the table
C 0x4A51 The new-request marker
C 0x4A5D Six more bytes: the other part of the card
C 0x4A64 And seven: the next channel's card
L 0x4A6D load_explosion_graphics   With banks 4/5/6 in place, the entries at 0x98A3 and the mirror at 0x98B0; then clears the 0x800 bytes of objects
C 0x4A6D Banks 4, 5 and 6, which are the graphics ones
C 0x4A6E The three graphics banks
C 0x4A86 The entries at 0x98A3
C 0x4A8F 0xE100 to one: the byte mirror
C 0x4A92 And the mirror at 0x98B0
C 0x4A99 1, 2 and 3 restored
C 0x4A9A And 1, 2 and 3 restored
C 0x4AB2 And the 0x800 bytes of objects, to zero
C 0x4AB8 0x800 bytes
L 0x4AC0 dispatch_ship_end   0xE1D0 holds the explosion step and 0xE1D1 the submode: ten destinations in the table at 0x4ACA
C 0x4AC0 0xE1D0 the step and 0xE1D1 the submode
C 0x4AC5 With the step at zero there is no explosion
D 0x4ACA 0x4ADE dispatcher_table_4AC7  Ten words right behind the `call 0x4067` at 0x4AC7.
F 0x4ACA w2
L 0x4ADE explosion_step_0   Raises the counter at 0xE1D3 by 0x40 and, past Y 0xF0, clears the screen and turns the sprites off
C 0x4AE1 0x40 more each frame
C 0x4AE8 0x0800 in 0xE008
C 0x4AEE And until 0xE206 goes past 0xF0, nothing is cleared
L 0x4AFA next_submode   0xE1D1 + 1: the next step of the explosion
L 0x4AFF explosion_step_1   Waits for the channel to go quiet, loads the ending graphics from bank 10, turns the sprites off and puts up the 4x4 drawing
C 0x4AFF Until the 0xE012 channel goes quiet, it does not go on
C 0x4B07 Bank 10 at 0xA000: the ending graphics
C 0x4B11 Three thirds of patterns at VRAM 0x2418...
C 0x4B1A ...and three of colours at 0x0418
C 0x4B23 And the sprite patterns, at 0x1800
C 0x4B2C Bank 3 restored
C 0x4B39 The four-by-four drawing at 0x4FB2
C 0x4B43 0x00C0 in 0xE1D5: the long count
C 0x4B4E Sound 0x38
L 0x4B55 turn_off_sprites   Sets the 128 bytes at 0xEC80 to 0xE0, which is the Y at which a sprite cannot be seen.
C 0x4B58 The 128 bytes of the attribute table
L 0x4B60 explosion_step_2   Moves the objects until 0xE1D5 reaches zero, and then starts the drawing blinking
C 0x4B6C 0xE1D5 goes down elsewhere; until it reaches zero, it does not go on
C 0x4B71 0x10 frames per step of the drawing
C 0x4B7A Sound 0x3B
L 0x4B82 explosion_step_3   Every 0x10 frames swaps the 4x4 drawing for the next one at 0x6340; at the third, erases it and waits 0x40 frames
C 0x4B8B 0xE1D2 counts the frames of each drawing
C 0x4B93 0xE1D9 says which drawing it is on
C 0x4B98 Three drawings and it is over
C 0x4B9F The strip at 0x6340, in bank 1
C 0x4BA2 Times sixteen: four by four characters
C 0x4BAB Row 5, column 14
C 0x4BB8 0x1C: the row below, minus what has already been walked
C 0x4BC1 And the erasing
C 0x4BCB 0x40 frames of waiting
C 0x4BD0 Sound 0x44
L 0x4BD8 explosion_step_4   Lights up the six cells of the power-up meter one by one, each faster than the one before
C 0x4BE1 0xE1D2 counts the frames of this cell
C 0x4BE7 0xE1D9 says which cell it is on
C 0x4BEC Six cells and it is over
C 0x4BF0 0x4C10 is the speed table, and its first byte falls on top of the code.
C 0x4BF6 Each cell lasts less: 0x28, 0x28, 0x10, 0x0C, 0x08 and 0x04 frames
L 0x4BFD draw_meter   Takes from 0xE1D9 the lit cell of the power-up meter and writes its drawing with 0x4998.
C 0x4BFD 0xE1D9 says which cell of the meter is lit.
C 0x4C03 And it is drawn: 0x4998 writes characters, it does not execute anything.
L 0x4C06 finish_meter   Turns the sprites off and leaves 0x20 frames for the next step
D 0x4C10 0x4C16 speeds  Six bytes that 0x4BF0 indexes with 0x4062 and stores in 0xE1D2: 0x28, 0x28, 0x10, 0x0C, 0x08, 0x04.
F 0x4C10 6
L 0x4C16 explosion_step_5   Waits the frames in 0xE1D2 and then requests screen piece 0
C 0x4C1E Black border
C 0x4C23 And another 0x28 frames
B 0x4C2B THE CARTRIDGE CHECKS WHICH COUNTRY THE MACHINE IS FROM
B 0x4C2B At the end of a whole loop a message is written, and which one comes out
B 0x4C2B depends on which loop it is: GOOD, NICE, FINE and GREAT for the first
B 0x4C2B four. On the fifth, the cartridge reads 0x002B of the BIOS (the low
B 0x4C2B nibble gives the machine's character set, and at zero it is Japanese)
B 0x4C2B and splits: on a Japanese machine YOROKONDE ITADAKEMASHITAKA comes out,
B 0x4C2B and on any other, CONGRATULATIONS. Both messages are in the ROM, one
B 0x4C2B next to the other.
L 0x4C2B explosion_step_6   Clears the screen, raises the loop number in 0xE070 wrapping at five, and writes the message that is due
C 0x4C31 0xE070 is the loop: five and back to zero
C 0x4C3E Only on the fifth loop is the machine checked
C 0x4C42 The low nibble of 0x002B: the character set. At zero, Japanese
C 0x4C4B On any other machine, CONGRATULATIONS
C 0x4C50 And otherwise, the message for the loop: GOOD, NICE, FINE, GREAT or the Japanese one
C 0x4C5A Sound 0xA9
L 0x4C62 explosion_step_7   When the message is done, leaves 0x6001 in 0xE044 and writes the one at 0x5040
C 0x4C66 0x6001 in 0xE044 and 0xE045
C 0x4C6D 0xE046 to zero
C 0x4C70 And the message at 0x5040
L 0x4C79 explosion_step_8   When it is done, gives away 500 points
C 0x4C7D 0x0500 in BCD; the message at 0x5040 announces it as BONUS 50000 POINTS
L 0x4C86 explosion_step_9   Waits for the channel to go quiet, clears the screen and leaves both scripts pointing to 0x504A
C 0x4C86 Until 0xE012 goes quiet, it does not go on
C 0x4C90 0xE150 to one
C 0x4C93 0x504A: the script both lists start with
C 0x4C9E The two pointer tables, the one at 0xE224...
C 0x4CA4 ...and the one at 0xE244
C 0x4CAA And the 0x180 bytes of objects, to zero
L 0x4CB8 point_all_slots   Leaves the same pointer in the first slot and in the eight behind it
C 0x4CBC Ten bytes: the next slot
C 0x4CC0 Eight more slots, now two bytes apart
C 0x4CC2 The eight two-byte slots
B 0x4CC9 THE EXPLOSION SHRAPNEL, AND THE R REGISTER AGAIN
B 0x4CC9 When the ship blows up, a piece of shrapnel comes out every three frames,
B 0x4CC9 and all the chance in it comes from the Z80's R REGISTER, the memory
B 0x4CC9 refresh one, read four times in a row: one of its bits picks between the
B 0x4CC9 two drawings, another four pick one of the sixteen directions in the
B 0x4CC9 table at 0x4D3E, and the last two reads add a pinch to the X and to the
B 0x4CC9 Y so that two pieces with the same direction do not travel stuck
B 0x4CC9 together.
B 0x4CC9 In this mode, 0xE300 is NOT the usual object table: it is thirty-two
B 0x4CC9 slots of sixteen bytes.
L 0x4CC9 release_shrapnel   Every three frames releases a piece of shrapnel in the first free slot, with drawing, direction and push taken from the R register
C 0x4CC9 0xE1D7: one piece every three frames
C 0x4CD0 0xE1D5 is what is left of the explosion
C 0x4CDA The R register: one bit to pick between the two drawings
C 0x4CE2 The thirty-two shrapnel slots
C 0x4CE8 Sixteen bytes per slot
C 0x4CEB The first one that is at zero
L 0x4CF2 build_shrapnel_piece   Fills the slot: drawing, Y 0x38, X 0x80, and the direction it gets from the R register
C 0x4CF8 The starting Y, 0x38
C 0x4CFC And the X, 0x80: the centre
C 0x4CFF The R register again: four bits, one of the sixteen directions
C 0x4D03 Times four: two words per direction
C 0x4D14 With the other drawing, the speed is halved
C 0x4D18 Shifting with the sign: it works for the negative directions
C 0x4D20 And the R register once more, to scatter the X...
C 0x4D28 ...and once more for the Y
C 0x4D34 0x2C and 0x07: the pattern and the colour
C 0x4D39 0xE1D8 keeps count of the live pieces
D 0x4D3E 0x4D7E shrapnel_directions  Sixteen directions, two words each: the X step and the Y step, in 8.8 fixed point (0x0200 is two points per frame). They are indexed by 0x4D06 with four bits of the R register times four. They are the sixteen directions of the compass rose, with their signs.
F 0x4D3E w2
L 0x4D7E move_shrapnel   Walks the thirty-two sixteen-byte slots and adds its speed to each piece; any that goes off the screen is turned off
C 0x4D84 The slot at zero is free
C 0x4D8A The 16-bit X plus its speed
C 0x4D99 Past X 0xC0, the piece is gone
C 0x4DA0 And the same with the Y
C 0x4DB2 And the bottom limit, 0xF8
C 0x4DB6 The slot is freed...
C 0x4DBA ...and the count of live pieces goes down
C 0x4DBE Sixteen bytes: the next slot
L 0x4DC6 upload_shrapnel_to_buffer   The thirty-two slots to the attribute buffer: the Y, the X, the pattern and the colour; a free slot goes to 0xE0
C 0x4DCF Byte 3 is the Y
C 0x4DD8 With the slot free, 0xE0: off the screen
C 0x4DDC Byte 5 is the X
C 0x4DE1 And bytes 10 and 11, the pattern and the colour
C 0x4DEB Sixteen bytes: the next slot
L 0x4DF3 animate_shrapnel   Raises each piece's counter and changes its pattern depending on how many frames it has been in the air
C 0x4DFF One more frame for that piece
C 0x4E02 The first drawing has four phases; the other, two
L 0x4E13 four_phases   Changes pattern at 0x0C, 0x18 and 0x24 frames: 0x2C, 0x30, 0x34 and 0x38
C 0x4E13 The frames the piece has been in the air
C 0x4E16 Below 0x0C, the first pattern
C 0x4E1E And then 0x30, 0x34 and 0x38
L 0x4E2E two_phases   The other drawing only changes once, at 0x60 frames
C 0x4E2E The frames the piece has been in the air
D 0x4E3B 0x4E8E unreachable_code  Eighty-three bytes that disassemble as code (they start with `ld a,(0xE1D2) / dec a / ld hl,0x4E82`) but that no path reaches: the instruction before ends in `ret` and no instruction or table in the cartridge points to 0x4E3B. They are listed as bytes so as not to claim they are live code.
F 0x4E3B 16
L 0x4E8E pick_border_colour   A bit of 0xE1D2 picks between colour 3 and colour 9 for the border
C 0x4E8E A bit of 0xE1D2
L 0x4E9C start_slow_message   Stores the VRAM cell in 0xE1DB and the text in 0xE1DD: the message is going to be written letter by letter
C 0x4E9C The first two bytes are the cell
C 0x4EA0 The cell in 0xE1DB and the text in 0xE1DD
C 0x4EA7 0xE1DA to one: the first letter goes in straight away
L 0x4EAD write_one_letter   Every six frames writes ONE letter of the message; 0xFF ends it and 0xFE continues it at another cell
C 0x4EAD 0xE1DA: one letter every six frames
C 0x4EB4 How far the cell has got and how far the text has got
C 0x4EBC 0xFF: the message is complete
C 0x4EBF And 0xFE: it carries on at another cell
C 0x4ED0 The letter, to VRAM
C 0x4ED7 Exits with 0xFF: there is still message left
D 0x4EDA 0x4EE6 meter_table  Six words (0x4EE6, 0x4EEB, 0x4EF4, 0x4F08, 0x4F2B, 0x4F63) that 0x4BFD indexes with 0xE1D9, the lit cell of the power-up meter. They are NOT routines: they are the six drawings of the meter, and 0x4BFD ends in `jp 0x4998`, which is the one that writes characters.
F 0x4EDA w2
D 0x4EE6 0x4FB2 meter_drawings  The six drawings the table above points to, in the 0x4998 format: two bytes of VRAM address, followed by the characters (0xFE = another address follows, 0xFF = end). They are counted, not estimated: the six measure 5, 9, 20, 35, 56 and 79 bytes and fit exactly from 0x4EE6 to 0x4FB1. They write to the name table around 0x38EA-0x390E, which is the meter's row.
F 0x4EE6 16
D 0x4FB2 0x4FCE four_by_four_drawing  Four rows of four characters (0xEB to 0xF8) at row 5 and column 14, in the 0x4998 format: a drawing made of characters in the middle of the screen. Requested by 0x4B39.
F 0x4FB2 7
D 0x4FCE 0x4FEA erase_drawing  The same drawing but with zeros, that is, the erasing. Requested by 0x4BC1.
F 0x4FCE 7
D 0x4FEA 0x505A tables_of_4C4B  What 0x4C50 (0x4FEA), 0x4C4B (0x502E), 0x4C70 (0x5040) and 0x4C93 (0x504A) read.
F 0x4FEA 16
B 0x505A NEMESIS LOOKS FOR ANOTHER KONAMI CARTRIDGE IN THE OTHER SLOTS
B 0x505A At boot, INIT calls here and the cartridge starts looking at the
B 0x505A machine's other slots and subslots with RDSLT, reading SIX bytes
B 0x505A backwards from 0xBFFF and comparing them with the ones at 0x50AE.
B 0x505A Those six bytes (AC 81 91 06 40 AA read in memory order) are not just
B 0x505A any number: they are the END OF THE HIDDEN KONAMI MARK of another
B 0x505A cartridge, the same one Nemesis carries at the end of its bank 3.
B 0x505A Decoded with the house code: 0xAA closes it, 0x40 is the catalogue
B 0x505A number (RC-740), 0x06 says the title has six characters, and the three
B 0x505A being compared are, read the right way round, TU I N: ツイン, the
B 0x505A start of ツインビー. In other words, Nemesis is looking for TwinBee
B 0x505A (Konami, RC-740).
B 0x505A If it finds it, 0xF0F4 stays at one, and with that the game loads extra
B 0x505A graphics: the block at 0x9963 in 0x428A and the entries at 0x938E and
B 0x505A 0x939B in 0x4326.
L 0x505A find_other_cartridge   Walks slots 0, 0x80, 0x84, 0x88 and 0x8C looking for Konami's RC-740 mark
C 0x505B 0xF0F4 to zero: no companion for now
C 0x505E Slot 0 and the four subslots of slot 3
L 0x5078 try_four_subslots   Tries that slot and the next three; as soon as one matches, the search stops
C 0x5078 If it was already found, the search does not go on
C 0x507D Four subslots
L 0x507F try_one_subslot   Tries that subslot and moves on to the next one
C 0x507F Four subslots
C 0x5085 The next one
L 0x508D compare_mark   Reads six bytes backwards from 0xBFFF in that slot and compares them with the ones at 0x50AE
C 0x508D Six bytes are read from the end of page 2 of ANOTHER slot, backwards.
C 0x5090 And they are compared with the six at 0x50AE, which are the hidden Konami mark of another cartridge.
C 0x5093 Six bytes
C 0x5099 RDSLT: read an address from the slot A says, without switching slots.
C 0x50A2 As soon as one does not match, it gives up.
C 0x50A3 One goes up and the other goes down: the mark is written backwards
C 0x50A7 If all six match, 0xF0F4 = 1, and that switches on extra graphics.
D 0x50AE 0x50B4 rc740_mark  The six bytes 0x508D looks for in the other slots: AC 81 91 06 40 AA. They are the end of a hidden Konami mark: 0xAA closes it, 0x40 is the RC-740, 0x06 the length of the title, and AC 81 91 are, read the right way round, TU I N (ツイン), that is, TwinBee.
F 0x50AE 6
L 0x50B4 clear_typing_state   0xE1E0 to zero and the eight bytes at 0xE1E8 behind it
C 0x50BF Eight bytes
C 0x50C1 Eight bytes to zero
B 0x50C9 THE CHEATS TYPED ON THE KEYBOARD, WHILE PAUSED
B 0x50C9 This is NOT read while playing: 0x4518 only calls here when bit 0 of
B 0x50C9 0xE10B is set, that is, with the game PAUSED. You pause with the GRAPH
B 0x50C9 key (row 6, bit 5, at 0x44F6), type, and on unpausing the game carries
B 0x50C9 on. On entering pause, 0x50B4 erases whatever was typed before.
B 0x50C9 Each new key is stored in 0xE1E8, up to eight, and on pressing RETURN
B 0x50C9 what was typed is compared against the words at 0x51BF. What is in ROM:
B 0x50C9   HYPER    only the first time (0xE06E), and jumps to 0xA0D8:
B 0x50C9            EVERYTHING at once
B 0x50C9   LASER    -> 0xA101      MISSILE -> 0xA106
B 0x50C9   SHIELD   -> 0xA0E9      DOUBLE  -> 0xA10B
B 0x50C9   OPTION   -> 0xA110      DOWN    -> 0xA0FA, which takes away
B 0x50C9   BAKA and AHO (fool and idiot in Japanese) give NOTHING: they fall
B 0x50C9   into 0x5127, which zeroes the lives, the flag and the two joystick
B 0x50C9   flags.
B 0x50C9 And on top of that each stage has ITS own woman's name (MOMOKO, CHIE,
B 0x50C9 AKEMI, SYUKO, CHIAKI, NORIKO, SATOE, YASUKO, KINUYO, HISAE, MIYUKI,
B 0x50C9 YOHKO), which the table at 0x5163 hands out by stage: getting right
B 0x50C9 the one for the stage you are on sets 0xE071 to one and jumps to 0xA0D8.
B 0x50C9 The six prizes are only given if 0xE071 is still at zero, so the name
B 0x50C9 and the words get in each other's way.
B 0x50C9 MEASURED IN openMSX with tools/omsx_cheats.tcl, not deduced: paused,
B 0x50C9 0xE1E8 fills with 4F 50 54 49 4F 4E (OPTION) and on pressing RETURN
B 0x50C9 0xE20B goes from 00 to 02, the two options. With BAKA, the lives at
B 0x50C9 0xE060 go from 02 to 00 and the 0xE05F flag goes off.
L 0x50C9 check_typed_keys   Reads the keyboard and, on RETURN, compares what was typed against the cheats; each one jumps to its own routine in bank 3
C 0x50C9 With 0xE200 negative there is no ship, and no cheats
C 0x50CE Which key is being pressed
C 0x50D3 0xE1E1 keeps the previous one: only a new key counts
C 0x50DA 0x0D is RETURN: until it is pressed, keys are just written down
C 0x50DF 0x50B4 is pushed: whatever happens, what was typed is erased on the way out
C 0x50E3 HYPER
C 0x50E8 BAKA...
C 0x50ED ...and AHO: both of them punish
C 0x50F2 With 0xE071 already set no other prize is accepted
C 0x50F7 The woman's name that belongs to this stage
C 0x5102 MISSILE
C 0x5108 LASER
C 0x510E SHIELD
C 0x5114 DOUBLE
C 0x511A DOWN
C 0x5120 OPTION
L 0x5127 punish_insult   BAKA and AHO end up here: lives, flag and joystick flags to zero
C 0x5127 Lives, flag and the two joystick flags
L 0x5135 cheat_hyper   HYPER only works once per game, and 0xE06E keeps track of it
C 0x5135 0xE06E: HYPER only works once per game
L 0x513F cheat_stage_name   The stage's name guessed right: 0xE071 to one and off to bank 3
L 0x5147 record_key   Stores the new keys in 0xE1E8, up to eight
C 0x514B Eight letters at most
L 0x5157 compare_stage_name   Takes from the table at 0x5163 the name that belongs to this stage and compares it
D 0x5163 0x517F texts_per_stage  Fourteen words that 0x5157 indexes with the stage minus one: where the name typed on each stage starts (0x51F6, 0x51FD, 0x5202, ...).
F 0x5163 w2
L 0x517F compare_baka
C 0x517F BAKA
L 0x5184 compare_aho
C 0x5184 AHO
L 0x5189 compare_laser
C 0x5189 LASER
L 0x518E compare_shield
C 0x518E SHIELD
L 0x5193 compare_down
C 0x5193 DOWN
L 0x5198 compare_option
C 0x5198 OPTION
L 0x519D compare_double
C 0x519D DOUBLE
L 0x51A2 compare_missile
C 0x51A2 MISSILE
