L 0x52B3 state_machine   Eight states, dispatched by the table at 0x52C7.
C 0x52B3 0xE003 counts the runs of the interrupt.
C 0x52B7 This is the address the state is going to return to...
C 0x52C3 ...and it is pushed onto the stack ONLY if 0xE000 is less than 3.
C 0x52C4 Eight states, and the table goes right here behind.
D 0x52C7 0x52D7 state_table  Eight words right behind the `call 0x4067` at 0x52C4: the game's eight states (0x52D7, 0x5308, 0x5312, 0x537D, 0x53B1, 0x5464, 0x546F, 0x549A).
F 0x52C7 w2
L 0x52D7 state_0   Three substates: the message at 0x57BD, the 0xE004 wait and building the title screen
C 0x52D7 To substate 1 if it is not 0
C 0x52EC To substate 2 if it is not 1
C 0x52EE 0xE004: the frames of waiting left
C 0x52F3 And the title screen is built
C 0x52FA The wipe, the clean screen and the score
L 0x5308 state_1   Runs 0xE004 down and goes to the intro
L 0x5312 state_2   Maps banks 9 and 10 for one call, and splits into four substates
C 0x5314 Banks 9 and 10, which are the intro ones
C 0x532B And 2 and 3 restored
C 0x5345 While the 0xE012 channel is sounding, it waits
L 0x534C state_2_sub1   Waits for the joystick and starts the thing at 0x5C88
C 0x534E With the joystick untouched, it exits through p
C 0x5351 With the joystick untouched it exits through p
L 0x5357 state_2_sub2   Waits for the joystick and, if there is no flag in 0xE05F, goes back to state 0
C 0x5359 One step of the demo
L 0x5361 back_to_state_0   State 0, substate 0 and 0x20 frames of waiting
L 0x536C state_2_sub3   Waits for the joystick and starts the thing at 0x5BF8
C 0x536C The joystick again
L 0x5373 wait_thirty_two   0x20 frames in 0xE004 and on to the next substate
C 0x5375 The frames of waiting that A brings
L 0x5378 next_substate   0xE001 + 1
L 0x537D state_3   Makes the message at 0x5808 or the one at 0x5812 blink (depending on bit 5 of 0xE002) by writing it and erasing it
C 0x5385 Bit 5 of 0xE002 picks between the two messages
C 0x5392 A bit of the counter: it is written and erased, and that is the blinking
L 0x539A state_3_sub1   Calls 0x5558 and goes to the next state
L 0x539F wait_thirty_two_then_state   0x20 frames and on to the next state
L 0x53A4 next_state   0xE000 + 1 and the substate to zero
L 0x53AD state_3_sub2   0x50 frames of waiting
L 0x53B1 state_4   Erases the message at 0x5820, moves on to the next stage and turns on the 0xE05F flag
C 0x53B1 To substate 1 if it is not 0
L 0x53C8 state_4_sub1   Takes away a life in BCD, maps banks 4/5/6 and decompresses the six blocks of the stage screen
C 0x53CC 0xE060 is the lives, in BCD
C 0x53D7 Banks 4, 5 and 6
C 0x53D8 Banks 4, 5 and 6 to read the stage graphics
C 0x53F0 Three pattern blocks: 0x3058, 0x30A8 and 0x3160
C 0x540B And their three colour ones: 0x1058, 0x10A8 and 0x1160
C 0x5426 1, 2 and 3 restored
C 0x5427 The usual layout again
C 0x543F Six bytes at 0xE131, to zero
C 0x5452 Bit 5 of 0xE002 says whether there are two players
C 0x5457 With one player, 0x10 frames
C 0x545F And with two, 0x78: there is time to read whose turn it is
L 0x5464 state_5   The game frame with the pause; while the 0xE05F flag is set, it does not leave
L 0x546F state_6   With no lives left sound 0xCA is requested; if the other player still has some, the turn changes
C 0x546F 0xE060 is the lives of the one playing
C 0x5475 And 0xE090 the other one's
L 0x547B switch_player   Swaps the 0x30 bytes at 0xE060 with those at 0xE090 and flips the turn bit
C 0x547B The 0x30 state bytes of the two players are swapped
C 0x548A And bit 7 of 0xE002 is inverted: the turn changes
C 0x548D State 4: on to setting up the stage
C 0x5492 Sound 0xCA: the end of the game
L 0x549A state_7   The end: waits for the sound and, if continue has been requested, gives three lives back and carries on
C 0x549F Until 0xE012 goes quiet, it does not go on
C 0x54A4 0xE06F: continue has been requested
C 0x54AD Bit 7 of 0xE002 says which player the score belongs to
C 0x54B6 The four bytes of the score, to zero
C 0x54BE And three lives again
C 0x54C3 If the other player has some left, the turn changes
C 0x54CF And if not, bit 6 of 0xE002 is turned off and on to the intro
L 0x54D9 state_7_sub1   Writes the message at 0x5836 and waits for continue to be requested
C 0x54D9 With the joystick untouched, it exits through p
L 0x54F3 check_continue_request   Bit 1 of keyboard row 7: when it is pressed, 0xE06F to one and the message at 0x5843 is erased
C 0x54F5 SNSMAT of row 7
C 0x54F8 Not pressed, the bit is at one
L 0x5506 states_return   The states return here: 0x52B7 pushes this address onto the stack before dispatching.
C 0x5509 0xE051 and 0xE052: what was pressed in the intro
C 0x551D Bits 4 and 5: one or two players
C 0x5521 With one player, 0x40 in 0xE002; with two, 0x60
C 0x552C State 3
C 0x5536 With no choice made, sound 0xCD
C 0x5540 And the bit of 0xE052 is inverted: the choice changes
L 0x5545 write_turn   With two players, the message at 0x5820 or the one at 0x582B depending on whose turn it is
C 0x5548 Bit 5 of 0xE002: only with two players
C 0x554E And bit 7 says which of the two
L 0x5558 start_whole_game   Clears the 0xFA9 bytes at 0xE057, leaves three lives and a flag, the prize at 0x0010, and with two players copies everything to the second one
C 0x5558 The 0xFA9 bytes from 0xE057, to zero
C 0x5565 Three lives and the flag, from 0x5588
C 0x5570 0x0010 in 0xE067: the first extra-life prize
C 0x5576 With two players...
C 0x557C ...the 0x30 state bytes are also copied to the second one
D 0x5588 0x558A two_bytes_to_e060  Two bytes (0x03, 0x01) that 0x5565 copies to 0xE060 with LDIR.
F 0x5588 2
L 0x558A lower_curtain   Lowers 0xE004 and, each frame, clears one row of the screen from bottom to top; exits with the sign set when it is over
C 0x558D On going below zero, the wipe is over
C 0x5590 The row comes from 0xE004 reversed: 0x1F minus the count
C 0x5595 Twenty-two rows high
C 0x5597 In state 2 it is twenty-four
C 0x55A1 0x20: the next row
L 0x55AA remove_sprites_from_screen   Writes 0xD0 at VRAM 0x3B00: the attribute that tells the VDP that the sprites end there.
L 0x55B4 add_to_score   Adds in BCD (it uses `daa`) onto the score of the player 0xE002 says.
C 0x55B4 Bit 6 of 0xE002 says whether there is a game
C 0x55B9 0xE05C is player 1's score and 0xE058 player 2's
C 0x55C2 The `daa`: the score is in BCD, three bytes
C 0x55D1 Past six nines, it sticks at 999999
C 0x55DE 0xE067 is the next extra-life prize
C 0x55E2 DCOMPR: compares the score with the prize
C 0x55E5 0x1000 more in BCD: the next prize
C 0x55F8 And from here down, the high score
C 0x5603 Three bytes, from highest to lowest
C 0x5609 If the score goes past the high score...
C 0x5613 ...the high score is copied whole
L 0x561C award_ship   Adds a life in BCD, and if there is no explosion in progress the alert sounds
C 0x561F One more, in BCD
C 0x5623 Past ninety-nine nothing is given away
C 0x5625 With an explosion in progress, it does not sound
C 0x562A Sound 0x15
L 0x5632 write_captions   Writes with 0x4998 the messages at 0x57CE, 0x57E1 and 0x57E6.
C 0x5638 Bit 7 of 0xE002 picks between the 1P and the 2P label
L 0x564D draw_scores   The high score on row 23 and the score of the player that is due, three BCD bytes each
C 0x564D 0xE056: the high score
C 0x5650 Row 23, column 22
C 0x5656 And the score of the player whose turn it is
C 0x5662 0xE05A if it is the second one
L 0x5668 draw_ships   The lives digit; with fewer than ten the tens digit is turned off
C 0x5668 Row 23, column 3
C 0x5671 With the high nibble at zero only one digit is painted
C 0x5673 Row 23, column 3: the lives digit
C 0x5679 The data port, which 0x494A left in the alternate C
L 0x5682 write_digits   Sends the BCD digits out of the data port, from lowest to highest and two per byte
C 0x5687 And the same count for the digits of the score
C 0x5689 The high nibble...
C 0x5693 ...and the low one
C 0x5696 Plus one: the zero character is not 0
L 0x569D draw_power_up_meter   The six cells of the meter on row 22, each with its four characters
C 0x569D Row 22, column 4
C 0x56A3 Six cells from 0xE131
C 0x56A9 Six meter cells
L 0x56B2 draw_meter_cell   The four characters of that cell, off or on, and from another set if it is the selected one
C 0x56B3 Times eight: eight bytes per cell
C 0x56BE With the byte at zero, the cell is off
C 0x56C4 And 0xE130 says which one is selected: that one gets the other four
C 0x56CE Four characters per cell
D 0x56D1 0x5709 pairs_of_5709  Rows of eight bytes that 0x56B6 indexes with the value times eight.
F 0x56D1 8
C 0x56D1 The character, through the data port
D 0x5709 0x5711 alternative_row  The row of eight bytes that 0x56C1 switches to when the previous byte is not zero.
F 0x5709 8
L 0x5711 swap_b_bytes   Swaps B bytes between HL and DE, one by one
C 0x5711 B bytes, one by one
L 0x571B cell_to_ram_address   From the screen cell in HL works out the address in the RAM map: row times 0x20 plus column, on top of 0xED00
C 0x5720 Four shifts of the whole of HL: divided by sixteen
C 0x5729 The two bits that are left...
C 0x572B ...plus 0xED: the map lives from 0xED00 to 0xEFFF
L 0x572F start_machine   Silences the PSG, requests sound 0xCD, clears the 16 KB of VRAM and programs the eight registers
C 0x572F 0xB8 in PSG register 7: the three channels silenced
C 0x573A Sound 0xCD
C 0x573F The 0x4000 bytes of VRAM, to zero
B 0x5749 THE CARTRIDGE TURNS THE VRAM MAP UPSIDE DOWN
B 0x5749 The eight bytes at 0x575A are 0x02, 0xE2, 0x0E, 0x7F, 0x07, 0x76, 0x03
B 0x5749 and 0xE4, and they do not leave VRAM where the BIOS leaves it. Register 4
B 0x5749 is 0x07, so the PATTERNS live at 0x2000; register 3 is 0x7F, so the
B 0x5749 COLOURS live at 0x0000 (exactly the reverse of normal); register 2 is
B 0x5749 0x0E, which puts the name table at 0x3800; register 5 is 0x76, which
B 0x5749 puts the sprite attributes at 0x3B00, right behind the names; and
B 0x5749 register 6 is 0x03, which puts the sprite patterns at 0x1800. Anyone
B 0x5749 who reads the addresses in this listing expecting the BIOS layout will
B 0x5749 get every one of them wrong.
L 0x5749 program_vdp   The eight values at 0x575A to registers 0 to 7, with WRTVDP
C 0x574C Eight registers
D 0x575A 0x5762 vdp_registers  The eight values with which 0x5749 programs VDP registers 0 to 7, one after the other, calling WRTVDP (0x0047) with B = the value and C = the register number. From them follow: patterns at 0x2000, colours at 0x0000, names at 0x3800, sprite attributes at 0x3B00 and sprite patterns at 0x1800.
F 0x575A 8
L 0x5762 set_border_colour   Writes B to VDP register 7: the border and background colour
L 0x5767 read_controller   Combines joystick and keyboard into 0xE009 and leaves in 0xE008 what has just been pressed
L 0x576D just_pressed   Saves the current state and, with an xor and an and, leaves in the next byte only what has just gone down
C 0x576D What has changed and is set: what was just pressed
L 0x5774 read_controller_no_save   The same but without recording it: used from the states' return point
C 0x5779 The six low bits: four arrows and two fire buttons
L 0x577D merge_joystick_and_keyboard   Reads the joystick on port 1 through the PSG and adds the keys of rows 4 and 8, shifted to the same bits
C 0x5787 SNSMAT of row 4
C 0x578B Its bits 2 and 3
L 0x5792 merge_row_eight   Takes the arrows and the space bar from keyboard row 8 and shifts them to the joystick's bits
C 0x5792 SNSMAT of row 8
C 0x5794 And row 8, the one with the arrows and the space bar
C 0x5798 Shifted twice: the bits land where the joystick's do
C 0x579B The space bar bit
C 0x57A3 The two middle arrows
C 0x57A9 And the other two
L 0x57AF read_joystick   PSG register 15 selects the port and 14 reads it; it arrives inverted, hence the cpl
C 0x57B1 Register 15 selects the port
C 0x57B7 And 14 brings the arrows and the fire buttons
C 0x57BB A cpl: in the PSG a pressed button is a zero
D 0x57BD 0x5851 messages  The screen texts, with THEIR OWN CHARACTER CODES: 0x00 is the space, 0x10 to 0x19 are the digits 0 to 9 and 0x21 to 0x3A the letters (that is, ASCII minus 0x20). They are read with two different routines, and both start by taking the VRAM address from the stream itself: 0x4998 writes character by character (0xFE = another address follows, 0xFF = end) and 0x49B3 decompresses them with the 0x49B9 format. That is where "software", "konami", "1986", "play select", "1 player", "2 players", "player 1", "player 2", "game over" and "continue" come from. Each message starts where its caller asks for it: 0x57BD (0x52E2, compressed), 0x57CE (0x5632), 0x57E1 (0x563B), 0x57E6 (0x5641), 0x57EB (0x5BCA), 0x5808 (0x538A), 0x5812 (0x538F), 0x581D (0x5BF1), 0x5820 (0x53B8 and 0x554B), 0x582B (0x5552) and 0x5836 (0x54E0).
F 0x57BD 16
L 0x5851 load_scoreboard   Dumps into VRAM 0x2080 and 0x2100 the patterns at 0x5906 and 0x596E, across the three thirds.
C 0x5851 The 104 bytes at 0x5906: the digits and letters of the score
C 0x585D And the 216 at 0x596E, which are the rest of the font
C 0x5869 0xF0 in 0x158 colour cells: white on transparent
L 0x5874 load_scoreboard_two_thirds   The same as 0x5851 but only in the first and second thirds, and this time one at a time
C 0x5897 And again in the second third, at 0x2880
L 0x58BA load_stage_font   Sets 0x90 colour cells of the third third to white and uploads there the digits and the nine characters at 0x58FD
C 0x58BA 0x50 colour cells to white...
C 0x58C5 ...and 0x40 more
C 0x58D0 The digits, at VRAM 0x3008
C 0x58DC And from here down, the loose characters in the list at 0x58FD
L 0x58E6 upload_loose_characters   For each number in the list, its eight bytes from 0x596E; 0x00 ends it
C 0x58E8 The 0x00 ends the list
C 0x58EA Times eight: eight bytes per character
C 0x58F3 Eight bytes through the data port
D 0x58FD 0x5906 drawing_order  Nine bytes that 0x58E3 walks after setting VRAM to 0x3068.
F 0x58FD 9
D 0x5906 0x596E patterns_2080  One hundred and four raw bytes that 0x585A passes to 0x4964 with HL=0x2080: they are copied to the three thirds.
F 0x5906 8
D 0x596E 0x5A46 patterns_2100  Two hundred and seventeen raw bytes that 0x5866 passes to 0x4964 with HL=0x2100.
F 0x596E 8
L 0x5A46 start_logo_curtain   0x0E steps in 0xE00A and the cursor at row 21, column 10: that is where the logo starts rising from
C 0x5A48 Fourteen rows
C 0x5A4B Row 21, column 10
L 0x5A54 intro   Decompresses the logo at 0x5A9A into the three thirds of VRAM 0x6200 and sets its 0xD8 colours to white
C 0x5A63 0xF0: white on transparent
L 0x5A68 raise_logo   One row higher per call: paints the logo's three strips of characters and erases the one below
C 0x5A6B The cursor goes up 0x20 cells: one row
C 0x5A72 Three characters at the top, twelve in the middle and twelve at the bottom
C 0x5A84 And zeros underneath, which erase what the previous pass left
C 0x5A87 0xE00A counts the fourteen rows
L 0x5A8C character_strip   B consecutive characters starting at A, and then down one row
C 0x5A91 The character code goes up with the column
C 0x5A95 0x20: the row below, for the next call
D 0x5A9A 0x5B31 stream_6200  Compressed stream that 0x5A54 (the intro) passes to 0x4988 with HL=0x6200.
F 0x5A9A 16
L 0x5B31 build_title_screen   With banks 9 and 10 in place, black border, clean screen, the font and the two blocks of the title drawing
C 0x5B31 Banks 9 and 10: the intro ones
C 0x5B45 0xE0 in register 7: black border
C 0x5B50 The title patterns, at VRAM 0x2468...
C 0x5B59 ...and its colours, at 0x0468
C 0x5B62 2 and 3 restored
C 0x5B63 2 and 3 restored
L 0x5B77 write_title_panel   Builds the screen and writes on top of it the 28x5 panel that matches the machine's country
B 0x5B8E THE SAME CARTRIDGE IS CALLED GRADIUS OR NEMESIS DEPENDING ON THE MACHINE
B 0x5B8E Here is the reason this game has two names. The cartridge reads 0x002B
B 0x5B8E of the BIOS and keeps the low nibble, which is the machine's character
B 0x5B8E set: at zero, Japanese. And with that it picks which of the TWO logos
B 0x5B8E it writes on row 4: the one at 0x9BCB or the one at 0x9B3F, five rows
B 0x5B8E of 28 characters each, both in bank 9 and right next to each other.
B 0x5B8E Drawn from the ROM (decompressing their patterns from 0x9C57 and their
B 0x5B8E colours from 0x9EAB and painting the panel) you can read what they
B 0x5B8E say: the Japanese machine's one says GRADIUS and the other NEMESIS.
B 0x5B8E They are not two versions of the cartridge: it is the SAME binary, with
B 0x5B8E both titles inside.
B 0x5B8E It is the same split by country that 0x4C42 uses for the ending message.
C 0x5B8E The low nibble of 0x002B: the character set. At zero, Japanese
C 0x5B93 The Japanese panel...
C 0x5B98 ...and the one for the other machines
C 0x5B9B Row 4, column 2
C 0x5B9E Five rows
C 0x5BA4 Twenty-eight characters per row
C 0x5BA6 Twenty-eight characters, through the data port
C 0x5BAE 0x20: the row below
C 0x5BB0 0x20: the row below
C 0x5BCA And on top, the two messages at 0x57EB
L 0x5BD3 blink_selection   A bit of 0xE004 turns off and on the message of the option selected in the intro
C 0x5BD6 Bit 3 of the counter: on and off
C 0x5BDD Row 17, column 10, and the one 0x40 further down
C 0x5BE3 0xE052 says which of the two is selected
D 0x5BF7 0x5BF8 stray_byte  A 0x00 between two routines. GUESS: alignment filler; no instruction reads it.
F 0x5BF7 1
L 0x5BF8 build_high_score_screen   With banks 9 and 10, decompresses the six blocks of the high score table and copies the 768 characters at 0x8000 to it as they are
C 0x5C0F Three pattern blocks, at 0x2008, 0x2808 and 0x3008
C 0x5C2A And their three colour ones, at 0x0008, 0x0808 and 0x1008
C 0x5C45 The characters of the frame around it, in the three thirds
C 0x5C57 And the sprite patterns
C 0x5C66 0x8000: 768 UNCOMPRESSED characters, the whole screen in one go
C 0x5C83 Sound 0xA6
B 0x5C88 THE DEMO PLAYS ITSELF BY READING A RECORDING OF THE JOYSTICK
B 0x5C88 The demo is not played by any clever machine: it is a RECORDING. In bank
B 0x5C88 12 there is, per stage, a list of pairs [how many frames][what the
B 0x5C88 joystick reads], and 0x5CDA reads through it: 0xE00B counts the frames
B 0x5C88 left and 0xE00C holds the joystick value, which 0x5CCF puts in 0xE009
B 0x5C88 (the same byte where 0x5767 leaves what it reads from the real
B 0x5C88 joystick), as well as turning on bit 4, which is fire. So the demo ship
B 0x5C88 ALWAYS fires and moves the way the tape says.
B 0x5C88 And it starts fully powered: 0x5CB8 calls 0xA0D8, which is the same
B 0x5C88 thing the HYPER cheat gives.
L 0x5C88 start_demo   Picks the stage that is due (0xE006 wraps at eight), sets it up with all the power-ups and leaves it ready to play itself
C 0x5C90 0xE05F to one: the flag that says a demo is running
C 0x5C93 0xE006 holds which stage the demo is on
C 0x5C9B Eight stages and back to the first
C 0x5CA2 0x20 of distance covered
C 0x5CA8 0xE00B to one: the first step of the recording goes in straight away
C 0x5CB8 And all the power-ups at once, like the HYPER cheat
L 0x5CBE run_demo   While it lasts, takes the joystick value from the recording and passes it to the game as if someone had pressed it
C 0x5CBE With 0xE064 set, the demo is cut short
C 0x5CC9 0xE00B: the frames left in this step
L 0x5CCF feed_recorded_controller   The recorded value goes to 0xE009, with bit 4 (fire) always set
C 0x5CD2 Bit 4: in the demo it fires non-stop
L 0x5CDA next_recording_step   Maps banks 11 and 12, takes the next pair from the stage's list and leaves it in 0xE00B and 0xE00C
C 0x5CDC 0xE00D says which pair it is on
C 0x5CDE Banks 11 and 12: the recordings are there
C 0x5CF5 The table at 0x5D1D, indexed by the stage
C 0x5CFE Times two: each step is two bytes
C 0x5D00 The first, the frames it lasts
C 0x5D05 And the second, what the joystick reads
C 0x5D09 2 and 3 restored
D 0x5D1D 0x5D41 ends_per_stage  Words that 0x5CF5 indexes with the stage (0x47AE), with the base 0x5D1D: 0xA878, 0xA942, 0xAA92, 0xAC34, 0xAD94, 0xAF54, 0xB078 and 0xB13A, all of them in bank 3.
F 0x5D1D w2
L 0x5D41 release_what_is_due   From the ninth stage onwards, and only on the steps with a new column, releases whatever the script says until it runs out
C 0x5D44 Below the ninth, no
C 0x5D47 And only on the steps that bring in a column
C 0x5D4C 0xF8 in 0xEC04
L 0x5D57 release_one   Checks whether something is due to be released here; if so, takes the position and the type from the script and calls the engine in bank 1
C 0x5D5B 0xE127 moves on to the next row of the script
C 0x5D60 The five high bits: the Y
C 0x5D6A Bit 2 chooses between two types
L 0x5D81 check_stage_script   With banks 11 and 12, looks in the stage script for the row that matches the distance covered and compares it
C 0x5D82 Banks 11 and 12: the scripts are there
C 0x5D95 The table at 0xB2D2, indexed by the stage
C 0x5D9F 0xE127: which row of the script it is on
C 0x5DA3 Three bytes per row
C 0x5DAA The first, the type; the other two, the distance
C 0x5DAF 2 and 3 restored
C 0x5DC3 DCOMPR: the distance covered against the row's
L 0x5DC8 run_twelve_objects   The twelve objects at 0xE300, 0x20 bytes apart: each one gets its mover routine and then 0x5F66
C 0x5DCC Twelve objects
C 0x5DD6 Thirty-two bytes per object
L 0x5DDE dispatch_object_mover   The first byte says what the object is; with the screen stopped (0xE1C0) there are three cases, and otherwise the table of thirty-one
C 0x5DE1 0xE1C0 non-zero: the screen is stopped
C 0x5DF8 Type 0 is the empty slot
D 0x5DFD 0x5E3B dispatcher_table_5DFA  Thirty-one words right behind the `call 0x4067` at 0x5DFA. It is the largest table in the cartridge, and almost all of its destinations are in ANOTHER bank: it sends to 0x8000 and 0xA000, that is, to banks 2 and 3.
F 0x5DFD w2
L 0x5E3E animate_four_drawings   The object's first sixteen frames cycle through the four character-and-colour pairs at 0x5E5D
C 0x5E41 One more frame for this object
C 0x5E44 Past sixteen, the animation is over
C 0x5E48 Skipping one bit: each drawing lasts two frames
C 0x5E52 The character in byte 12 and the colour in 13
D 0x5E5D 0x5E65 characters_of_5E4B  Eight bytes that 0x5E4B indexes with (A AND 6) and puts in (IX+0x0C).
F 0x5E5D 8
L 0x5E65 object_to_explosion   Turns the object into an explosion: gives it type 0x12 or 0x13 and its character, and snaps its X and Y to the grid
C 0x5E65 Byte 17 says whether the object belongs to a group
C 0x5E6E The group is looked up in the table at 0xE900
C 0x5E77 The group's count goes down; until it reaches zero, it does not blow up
C 0x5E88 Without a group, byte 14 decides
C 0x5E95 Type 0x12 or 0x13: the two explosion sizes
C 0x5E9D Byte 11 to one: it is drawn with characters
C 0x5EA5 The Y, snapped to eight, plus four
C 0x5EAF And the X, snapped to eight
L 0x5EBA find_group   Walks the four three-byte entries at 0xE900 looking for group A; exits with carry if it is not there
C 0x5EBD Four groups
C 0x5EC1 Three bytes per group
L 0x5EC8 animate_four_characters   The first sixteen frames, a character from 0x5EE3 every four; after that, the object blows up
C 0x5ECE Past sixteen, on to exploding
C 0x5ED2 Skipping two bits: each drawing lasts four frames
D 0x5EE3 0x5EE7 characters_of_5ED6  Four bytes (0xF0, 0xF4, 0xF8, 0xFC) that 0x5ED6 indexes with (A AND 3).
F 0x5EE3 4
L 0x5EE7 move_hatch_enemy   Every four frames changes drawing, moves with the screen and, when the ship crosses its row, turns
C 0x5EEA One frame in four
C 0x5EF4 Eight drawings, round and round
C 0x5F05 On the steps with a new column, it moves eight points to the left
C 0x5F13 0xE204 is the ship's row, and bit 7 of byte 8 the sign of its vertical speed
L 0x5F26 enemy_turns   Stops going up or down (the speed in bytes 7 and 8 to zero) and takes 0xFC00 horizontally: it launches itself at the ship
C 0x5F30 0xFC00 in bytes 9 and 10: four points per frame to the left
C 0x5F39 0xE066 is the stages played: from the second loop onwards, it fires when it turns
D 0x5F47 0x5F4F animation_of_5EF6  Eight bytes (4,5,6,7,8,7,6,5) that 0x5EF6 indexes with the counter (IX+2) AND 7: the back-and-forth of an animation.
F 0x5F47 8
L 0x5F4F check_map_collision   Passes the object's X and Y to bank 2, with a correction of eight depending on bit 0 of byte 8
C 0x5F55 Bit 0 of byte 8 says whether the Y has to be shifted
L 0x5F66 check_object_gone   Types 2 to 0x10 and 0x1B to 0x1D add their speed and, if they go off the screen, are turned off
C 0x5F69 Types 0 and 1 do not move this way
C 0x5F6C And neither does 0x1E
C 0x5F6F Nor do 0x11 to 0x1A
L 0x5F7C add_speed   Adds the object's two 16-bit speeds to it and returns whether it is still inside the screen
C 0x5F7F Bytes 3 and 4: the 16-bit Y
C 0x5F84 And 7 and 8: its speed
C 0x5F8E Past Y 0xB0 or X 0xF9, the object is gone
L 0x5F9A add_word   Adds two bytes from DE onto the two at HL, with carry
C 0x5F9A The low byte...
C 0x5F9F ...and the high one with the carry
L 0x5FA5 turn_off_object   Frees the slot and lowers the count of live objects; if it was in a group, lowers the group's count too
C 0x5FA8 Type 0x1E takes up three slots
C 0x5FAC 0xE126 keeps the count of live objects
C 0x5FB7 Bit 0 of byte 17: it belongs to a group
C 0x5FBB Bit 0 of byte 17: the object was in a group
C 0x5FBF Its group is looked up in the table at 0xE900
C 0x5FC3 The group's count goes down
C 0x5FC7 And on reaching zero, the group is closed
L 0x5FCA turn_off_three_slots   The big type 0x1E object takes up three consecutive slots: all three are turned off and the count drops by three
C 0x5FD4 Thirty-two bytes: the next slot
C 0x5FD7 Three slots
L 0x5FE4 run_background_objects   Stage 5 has its own engine in bank 3; the others walk eight or two objects at 0xE700
C 0x5FE7 Stage 5 goes another way
C 0x5FF3 Stage 3 has eight; the others, two
